a nil string is not a segmentation fault

CFAttributedStringCreate copies the string it is given, so nil reached CFStringCreateCopy and
killed the process with nothing named: iA Writer died that way four frames under
-[NSViewController view] and the fault address was the only clue.

A DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE: macOS raises NSInvalidArgumentException and this answers an empty
string, because the caller is usually filling in a label and losing the whole application over
one is the worse of the two failures. It is announced once per process rather than silently, so
the caller's bug stays findable.

--- a/src/NSAttributedString.m
+++ b/src/NSAttributedString.m
@@ -508,6 +508,29 @@
 
 - (id)initWithString:(NSString *)str attributes:(NSDictionary *)attrs
 {
+    /*
+     * A NIL STRING IS THE CALLER'S MISTAKE, and it must not be a segmentation fault.
+     *
+     * CFAttributedStringCreate simply copies the string, so nil reached CFStringCreateCopy and
+     * killed the process with nothing named: iA Writer died that way four frames under
+     * -[NSViewController view], and the fault address was the only clue.
+     *
+     * A DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE: macOS raises NSInvalidArgumentException here and this answers an
+     * empty string, because the caller is usually filling in a label and losing the whole
+     * application over one is the worse of the two failures. It is announced once per process
+     * rather than silently, so the caller's bug stays findable; CIDER_TRACE_APP prints the frames.
+     */
+    if (str == nil) {
+        static BOOL announced = NO;
+
+        if (!announced) {
+            announced = YES;
+            NSLog(@"-[%@ initWithString:attributes:]: nil string, using an empty one",
+                  NSStringFromClass([self class]));
+        }
+        str = @"";
+    }
+
     CFAttributedStringRef string = CFAttributedStringCreate(kCFAllocatorDefault, (CFStringRef)str, (CFDictionaryRef)attrs);
     _CFAttributedStringSetMutable(string, self == mutablePlaceholder);
     return (id)string;
